A '''cyborg''' (cybernetic organism) is an organism integrated with technological components that support or modify its functions. The term was introduced by Manfred Clynes and Nathan Kline in 1960 in the context of space travel.

== Origins and interpretations ==
Clynes and Kline's 1960 article considered technological alterations that could help humans adapt to space without requiring continual conscious regulation. The engineering idea draws on cybernetic feedback and homeostatic regulation. Donna Haraway's later cyborg metaphor interrogates conventional distinctions between human, animal and machine in social and cultural analysis. These interpretations serve different purposes and should not be treated as competing specifications of one biomedical device.

== Control and interface engineering ==
An illustrative closed-loop biohybrid system observes a physiological variable y, compares it with a target r and applies a bounded intervention u through an actuator. A controller may depend on error e=r−y, measured history and safety limits. Device delay, sensor noise, physiological variation and actuator saturation influence stability. Open-loop prostheses and assistive systems can integrate technology with organisms without implementing the same feedback architecture; identify the actual coupling.

== Evaluation and ethical boundaries ==
Assess performance under specified clinical or engineering conditions, including reliability, failure handling, biocompatibility, power supply, cybersecurity and user control. A functional benefit must be demonstrated by appropriate evidence, not inferred from the label cyborg. A person using an assistive device retains their own identity and autonomy; access, consent and privacy matter. An inforg concerns informational connectedness without necessarily requiring bodily integration, while organoid intelligence concerns experimental tissue–computational interfaces. Technological augmentation does not by itself establish increased intelligence or consciousness.

